? Which attribute combination exports a Rust function to C as `int32_t checksum(const uint8_t*, size_t)`?
- `pub fn checksum(data: &[u8]) -> i32`
* `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern "C" fn checksum(data: *const u8, len: usize) -> i32`
- `#[export] fn checksum(data: *const u8, len: usize) -> i32`
- `extern "Rust" fn checksum(data: &[u8]) -> i32`
> `extern "C"` picks the C calling convention; `no_mangle` keeps the symbol name; slices don't exist in C, so pass pointer + length.

? Why should an exported function take `*const u8` rather than `&u8` for a C pointer argument?
* C may pass NULL (or anything), and a Rust reference must never be null or dangling; the function must validate first
- References are slower
- `&u8` isn't FFI-safe on 64-bit targets
- Because of the borrow checker
> Creating an invalid reference is immediate UB. Take raw pointers at the boundary, check them, then convert.

? How is a nullable C function pointer `void (*cb)(void*)` represented in Rust?
- `*const fn(*mut c_void)`
* `Option<unsafe extern "C" fn(*mut c_void)>`
- `&dyn Fn(*mut c_void)`
- `Box<dyn FnMut(*mut c_void)>`
> Rust guarantees `Option<fn>` has the same representation as a nullable function pointer, with `None` as NULL.

? A no_std crate is built as a `staticlib` and linked into a C program. Which crate must provide the `#[panic_handler]`?
- The C program
- No one: staticlibs can't panic
* The staticlib itself, because it is the final Rust artifact
- `core`
> Any final artifact of Rust code needs exactly one panic handler. Calling C's `abort()` from it is a common choice when the host is a C program.

? What happens since Rust 1.81 if a panic tries to unwind out of an `extern "C"` function?
- It unwinds into C, which is undefined behaviour
* The process aborts
- The panic is silently swallowed
- It compiles only with `extern "C-unwind"`
> Unwinding across a C frame is UB, so Rust aborts instead. `extern "C-unwind"` explicitly opts into unwinding.

? In edition 2024, what does `safe fn c_millis() -> u32;` inside an `unsafe extern "C"` block mean?
- The C function is checked by the compiler for safety
* You assert that calling it can never cause UB, so callers don't need an `unsafe` block
- It may only be called from safe code
- It is a Rust function exported to C
> The `unsafe extern` block makes declaring foreign items an explicit promise; `safe` marks items whose calls need no further justification.

? You need a `&CStr` for a fixed message to pass to C. What's the simplest no-heap way?
- `CString::new("hello")`
* `c"hello"`
- `"hello".as_ptr()`
- `b"hello"`
> C-string literals (Rust 1.77) produce `&'static CStr` with the NUL included. `"hello".as_ptr()` is not NUL-terminated.

? C code wants to keep a Rust object without Rust allocating. What pattern fits?
- Return a `Box<T>` to C
- Return `&'static mut T` from a `static mut`
* Export the type's size and alignment; C provides storage, Rust initialises the object in place and returns an opaque pointer
- Copy the whole struct to C on every call
> Common in embedded C APIs. Validate null, size and alignment before writing into the storage.

? Which tool generates C headers from a Rust crate's exported items?
* cbindgen
- bindgen
- cargo-c
- rustdoc
> cbindgen writes headers for Rust APIs; bindgen goes the other way (Rust declarations from C headers).
